{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LJaC7D5qLW7bjSPwmSa7hM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Ways of Working (WOW)\n2\t\n3\tConventions for this codebase.\n4\t\n5\t## Core principles\n6\t\n7\t- **Pure FP** — no mutable `var`; state changes through pure functions, effects in `IO` at the edge.\n8\t- **Fail fast** — no swallowed errors, no continuing after invalid state; guard clauses and assertions for nontrivial assumptions.\n9\t- **Make illegal states unrepresentable** — opaque types, enums and smart constructors in Scala 3.\n10\t- **Outcomes in the return type** — `Option` and `Either` for expected failure; exceptions are defects.\n11\t- **A pure core** — the domain takes values and returns values; I/O, clock and randomness stay at the boundary.\n12\t\n13\t## Stack\n14\t\n15\t- **Scala 3.3.4** on **sbt**. Pin that version in `build.sbt`; do not probe Maven for a newer Scala.\n16\t- **Cats** `cats-core` **2.12.0** and **Cats Effect** `cats-effect` **3.5.7**.\n17\t- **munit** **1.0.3** for tests, with `munit-cats-effect` **2.0.0** for `IO` and `munit-scalacheck` **1.0.0** for properties.\n18\t- **YAML** via `org.virtuslab::scala-yaml` **0.3.0**. No other YAML library.\n19\t- Do not `curl` Maven metadata or search.maven.org to discover versions — use the pins above.\n20\t\n21\t## Quality\n22\t\n23\t- **TDD** — failing test first, watched failing, then made to pass.\n24\t- **BDD** — test names state the observable behaviour, so the suite reads as the spec.\n25\t- **One behaviour per test** — a failure names the rule that broke.\n26\t- **No mocks** — pass a function where a test needs a collaborator.\n27\t- **Diagrams** — Mermaid, not ASCII art.\n28\t\n29\t## Code style\n30\t\n31\t- Top-down file layout: high-level logic at the top, helpers at the bottom.\n32\t- Constants at the top of the file instead of magic numbers.\n33\t- Push conditionals out to the caller where it keeps the happy path flat.\n34\t- Remove the old code when replacing an implementation.\n35\t- One small commit per step.\n36\t\n37\t## Checks\n38\t\n39\t```sh\n40\tsbt compile      # the first reviewer\n41\tsbt test\n42\tsbt scalafmtAll\n43\t```\n44\t\n45\tThe compiler is the first quality gate; the tests cover what types cannot state. Both give the same verdict for the same source, every run.\n46\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9b2ec4d8-2a9f-499e-9187-8f4707e1313e","uuid":"16a81171-adf9-452d-893c-6ad9681596c9","timestamp":"2026-09-29T22:00:45.114Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/isaiasbartelborth/Projects/hivemind/todo-list-cli/develop/docs/rules/WOW.md","content":"# Ways of Working (WOW)\n\nConventions for this codebase.\n\n## Core principles\n\n- **Pure FP** — no mutable `var`; state changes through pure functions, effects in `IO` at the edge.\n- **Fail fast** — no swallowed errors, no continuing after invalid state; guard clauses and assertions for nontrivial assumptions.\n- **Make illegal states unrepresentable** — opaque types, enums and smart constructors in Scala 3.\n- **Outcomes in the return type** — `Option` and `Either` for expected failure; exceptions are defects.\n- **A pure core** — the domain takes values and returns values; I/O, clock and randomness stay at the boundary.\n\n## Stack\n\n- **Scala 3.3.4** on **sbt**. Pin that version in `build.sbt`; do not probe Maven for a newer Scala.\n- **Cats** `cats-core` **2.12.0** and **Cats Effect** `cats-effect` **3.5.7**.\n- **munit** **1.0.3** for tests, with `munit-cats-effect` **2.0.0** for `IO` and `munit-scalacheck` **1.0.0** for properties.\n- **YAML** via `org.virtuslab::scala-yaml` **0.3.0**. No other YAML library.\n- Do not `curl` Maven metadata or search.maven.org to discover versions — use the pins above.\n\n## Quality\n\n- **TDD** — failing test first, watched failing, then made to pass.\n- **BDD** — test names state the observable behaviour, so the suite reads as the spec.\n- **One behaviour per test** — a failure names the rule that broke.\n- **No mocks** — pass a function where a test needs a collaborator.\n- **Diagrams** — Mermaid, not ASCII art.\n\n## Code style\n\n- Top-down file layout: high-level logic at the top, helpers at the bottom.\n- Constants at the top of the file instead of magic numbers.\n- Push conditionals out to the caller where it keeps the happy path flat.\n- Remove the old code when replacing an implementation.\n- One small commit per step.\n\n## Checks\n\n```sh\nsbt compile      # the first reviewer\nsbt test\nsbt scalafmtAll\n```\n\nThe compiler is the first quality gate; the tests cover what types cannot state. Both give the same verdict for the same source, every run.\n","numLines":46,"startLine":1,"totalLines":46}}}
